body:
_🩺 Stability & Availability_ | _🟡 Minor_ | _⚡ Quick win_

**Any `File::open` error (not just missing file) is treated as "safe to remove."**

`let Ok(f) = fs::File::open(&lock_path) else { remove_package(...); return true; }` deletes the package on *any* open failure, not only `NotFound`. A transient error (e.g. `EMFILE`, permission hiccup) would cause GC to wrongly conclude "no lock file, safe to remove" and delete a package that may actually be locked/in-use elsewhere, since the open failure — not absence — is what's being (mis)interpreted as absence.





<details>
<summary>🛡️ Suggested fix</summary>

```diff
-    let Ok(f) = fs::File::open(&lock_path) else {
-        // No lock file means the package was never run under this protocol;
-        // it is safe to remove.
-        remove_package(base, id);
-        return true;
-    };
+    let f = match fs::File::open(&lock_path) {
+        Ok(f) => f,
+        Err(e) if e.kind() == io::ErrorKind::NotFound => {
+            // No lock file means the package was never run under this
+            // protocol; it is safe to remove.
+            remove_package(base, id);
+            return true;
+        }
+        Err(_) => return false, // conservatively skip on unexpected I/O errors
+    };
```
</details>

